As a Senior Lead, I see exactly where the bottleneck is. The "Search" API is likely using a standard JPA projection or a DTO that treats the PAYLOAD column as a simple String, which causes Jackson to escape it (adding those \" backslashes) when it serializes the final response.
To fix the "Search" API so it returns a clean JSON object instead of a string, you need to ensure the Search DTO uses the @JsonRawValue annotation, just like your entity does.
1. Update your Search Response DTO
When your Search API fetches multiple records, it probably uses a DTO (like CommonRequestDto). You must ensure the payload field in this DTO is annotated to prevent string escaping.
File: com.tcs.fincore.CommonRequestService.dto.CommonRequestDto.java
package com.tcs.fincore.CommonRequestService.dto;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonRawValue;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@Data
public class CommonRequestDto {
    private Long id;
    private String reqType;
    private String reqStatus;
    private String creatorId;
    private LocalDateTime reqDate;

    /**
     * This is the fix! 
     * Even if the data is a String in the database, 
     * this tells the Search API to render it as a JSON Object.
     */
    @JsonRawValue
    private String payload;

    private String targetId;
}

2. The Search Logic (Mapping)
In your CommonRequestServiceImpl, when you map your CommonReq entity to the CommonRequestDto, the mapping is straightforward because both hold the payload as a String.
public CommonRequestDto toDto(CommonReq entity) {
    CommonRequestDto dto = new CommonRequestDto();
    dto.setId(entity.getId());
    dto.setReqType(entity.getReqType().name());
    dto.setReqStatus(entity.getReqStatus().name());
    dto.setCreatorId(entity.getCreatorId());
    dto.setReqDate(entity.getReqDate());
    
    // Just pass the String; @JsonRawValue on the DTO 
    // handles the "un-stringifying" for the API response.
    dto.setPayload(entity.getPayload()); 
    
    dto.setTargetId(entity.getTargetId());
    return dto;
}

3. Why this solves the Fetching problem
When Jackson (Spring's JSON engine) builds the final response for your /api/lho-transfers/search endpoint:
 * Without @JsonRawValue: Jackson sees a String. It thinks: "I need to make sure this text is safe," so it adds quotes and escapes: "payload": "{\"batchId\":\"...\"}".
 * With @JsonRawValue: Jackson sees the annotation and thinks: "I will just drop this text into the JSON stream exactly as it is." Because your database already contains valid JSON text, the output becomes a perfect nested object: "payload": { "batchId": "..." }.
4. Implementation Check: The "Search" Method
Ensure your search method in the service returns a Page or List of the updated DTO:
@Override
public PaginatedResponseDto<CommonRequestDto> searchLhoTransfers(SearchRequest request) {
    Page<CommonReq> page = commonReqRepository.findAll(specs, pageable);
    
    List<CommonRequestDto> dtoList = page.getContent().stream()
            .map(this::toDto) // Using the updated DTO with @JsonRawValue
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
            
    return new PaginatedResponseDto<>(dtoList, page);
}

